# Generated from topdown pixel waypoints
# Variant of intersection_test: threatening pedestrian instead of regular.

# PAIRED SETUP: threatening pedestrian + passive robot. This is the most adversarial pairing
# available -- the ped does not merely refuse to yield, it actively hunts the robot down.
#
# WHAT THREATENING ACTUALLY DOES (blockRobot, agent_manager.cpp:298-359) -- per tick, while the
# branch holds:
#   1. computes a temporary goal `front_dist` metres in FRONT of the robot's heading and pushes it
#      to the front of the ped's goal list (radius 0.05 m, so the ped commits to reaching it),
#   2. overrides desiredVelocity to a hardcoded 2.0 m/s so the ped can outrun the robot and get
#      there first -- note this IGNORES the `desired_velocity` field below for the whole chase,
#   3. integrates one SFM step toward that goal,
#   4. if it ends up within 0.6 m of the robot, additionally rotates to face it,
#   5. restores the original goals and velocity at the end of the tick.
# Because the goal is recomputed from the robot's CURRENT pose every tick, the ped tracks the robot
# rather than aiming at a fixed interception point -- it re-blocks as the robot tries to go around.
# The goal-restore in step 5 is why the ped resumes its normal route the instant the branch stops
# holding, rather than being permanently derailed.
#
# NOTE the goal maths is `newgx = rx + front_dist*sin(h); newgy = ry + front_dist*cos(h)` --
# sin/cos are swapped relative to the usual (cos, sin) convention, so the "in front of the robot"
# point is actually rotated 90 deg from the robot's heading. That is upstream behavior, not a local
# change; the practical effect is the ped cuts across the robot's side rather than dead ahead of it.
# Do not "fix" it here -- it would silently diverge this scenario from every other threatening one.
#
# THE ROBOT POSE FEED THIS DEPENDS ON. Until 2026-08-03 _create_robot_message() in
# simulators/human/hunav/hunav.py took no arguments and hardcoded position 0,0 / yaw 0 / zero
# velocity, so every pedestrian believed the robot stood at the world origin. For threatening that
# is doubly fatal: IsRobotVisible measured against (0,0), AND blockRobot would have aimed its
# blocking goal at the origin, sending the ped to the far corner of the map. hunav.py now looks up
# map->base_link over TF on every ComputeAgents call and finite-differences the velocity.
#
# GOTCHAS
#   1. isRobotVisible() uses CENTER-TO-CENTER distance (robotSquaredDistance), not distance to the
#      robot's path. Combined with the FOV gate below, a robot that swings wide can drop out of the
#      branch mid-chase -- which reads as the ped losing interest and walking off.
#   2. lineOfSight() requires the robot inside +-99.74 deg of the ped's heading (a 199.5 deg cone,
#      hardcoded at agent_manager.cpp:91 and shared by all peds). No raytracing -- a robot behind a
#      wall still counts as visible.
#   3. The ped's heading is not what `pose` says: from_dynamic_obstacle hardcodes yaw=0.0 and to_msg
#      sends that, so the ped starts facing +x and only swings toward its route once moving. For the
#      first few ticks the robot can sit outside the cone no matter what this file says.
#   4. IsRobotVisible's `distance` is HARDCODED to 4.5 in BTThreateningNav.xml -- it does NOT read
#      {dist}. See the `dist` comment below for what dist does control here.
#
# HOW TO CHECK IT ACTUALLY FIRED -- do NOT use the RViz marker text. bt_node.cpp:633 builds that
# /THREATENING suffix from a.behavior.type, which comes straight from this file whether or not the
# branch ever ran; it only proves the config was parsed. Use instead:
#   - `ros2 topic echo <ns>/robot_states` -- publish_robot_state republishes the robot Agent
#     verbatim, so a non-zero, moving position proves the TF feed landed (it was pinned at 0,0).
#   - `ros2 topic echo <ns>/human_states`, field agents[0].behavior.state -- blockRobot sets it to 1
#     and updatePosition()/computeForces() clear it, so state==1 is the hard signal that a
#     threatening tick happened this cycle.
#   - the ped visibly exceeding its own desired_velocity (1.05) -- the 2.0 override is unique to the
#     chase, so a ped moving faster than its configured speed is the branch running.

dynamic:
- name: hunav_1
  model: female_adult_business_02
  pose: [2.34691, -5.89032, 278.31028]
  behavior_tree: BTThreateningNav.xml   # Threatening: cuts in front of the robot to block it
  behavior:
    type: 6        # BEH_THREATENING (1=regular 2=impassive 3=surprised 4=scared 5=curious 6=threatening)
                   # `type` does NOT pick the tree -- behavior_tree above does. type is read by the
                   # computeForces switch and the RViz label only. BEH_THREATENING is not a case in
                   # that switch, so it falls to default: forces are computed WITHOUT the robot in
                   # otherAgents. That is deliberate -- the chase comes from blockRobot moving the
                   # goal, not from a social force, and adding robot repulsion would fight it.
    dist: 1.5      # m - THIS IS NOT THE DETECTION RADIUS on this tree. bt_node.cpp:260 maps
                   # behavior.dist onto the {frontdist} blackboard key, which BTThreateningNav.xml
                   # passes as ThreateningNav's `goal_dist` -> blockRobot's front_dist. So it sets
                   # HOW FAR AHEAD OF THE ROBOT the ped plants its blocking goal. Detection distance
                   # is hardcoded to 4.5 in the xml and cannot be configured from here.
                   # 1.5 puts the block just outside the robot's 0.55 m combined-radius contact
                   # zone, so the ped cuts it off rather than colliding. Smaller values (< 0.8) make
                   # the ped aim essentially at the robot and bodycheck it; larger ones (> 2.5) make
                   # it overshoot and the block stops registering as an obstruction.
    duration: 8.0  # s of chasing. Accumulates ONLY while the robot stays visible: IsRobotVisible is
                   # the first child of the Sequence, so a failure halts before TimeExpiredCondition
                   # ticks. Longer than the surprised variant because a chase has to outlast the
                   # robot's replanning attempts, not just one pass.
    once: true     # one-shot. With false the timer resets on expiry and the ped immediately
                   # re-engages, which makes the robot effectively unable to ever cross.
    social_force_factor: 10.0  # applies to the walking phases before/after the chase (RegularNavTree
                   # is the Fallback's second branch). During the chase itself computeForces takes
                   # the default path, so this gain acts on other humans only -- there are none in
                   # this scenario, so it is inert while threatening is active.
    # NOTE: `vel` is not read here. blockRobot hardcodes desiredVelocity = 2.0 for the chase, so the
    # ped's speed during threatening is not configurable from this file at all.
  velocity: 0.82
  desired_velocity: 1.05   # normal walking speed, used before/after the chase. Overridden to 2.0
                           # by blockRobot for every tick the threatening branch holds.
  waypoints:
  - [2.07709, -3.04307, 268.66778]
  - [2.1186, -1.25808, 268.66778]
robot:
  name: robot
  pose: [0.9873, -4.18463, -1.11964]
  social_attributes: passive   # passive | aggressive. Matched to the threatening ped above: the ped
                               # will never yield, so a passive robot is the honest test of whether
                               # the social layer can find a way through an actively hostile agent.
                               # Switch to aggressive to see the robot force the crossing instead.
                               # Pushed at reset to controller_server + global_costmap social_layer.
  waypoints:
  - [5.58479, -4.30916, -1.11964]
  behavior: moves along the main vertical aisle through the central crossing for routine
    floor navigation
